{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310813,"nonce":"mxDViZOnG/4CKt6sq47svMO2z6Y=","title":"Visible-token streaming (llama-chat NUE_LLAMA_SILENT_STREAM=0) costs ~10 tok/s vs silent on a real TERMINAL, but only ~1.5% over a TCP ...","message":"llama-chat streams visible tokens off the decode thread via Arc\u003cChannel\u003cStreamMsg\u003e\u003e\n+ a writer thread (46806fd7). User saw STREAM ON 76.9 vs OFF 88.1 tok/s (732 tok,\nttft equal -\u003e gap is in DECODE).\n\nMEASURED (all A/B, NUE_PROFILE_DECODE for fwd_s):\n- Fast sink (file / pipe / even throttled 400 B/s pipe): NO gap (writer idle,\n  drains instantly; pipes have a 64KB buffer so no backpressure). detok\n  (decodeStreamStep) is NEGLIGIBLE (0.56ms / 150 tok).\n- Real TERMINAL: ~10 tok/s gap. The tty applies flow control (write() blocks)\n  + per-write render cost -\u003e the writer can't drain -\u003e bounded channel fills\n  -\u003e decode blocks on send. NOT reproducible headless (need a real tty).\n- TCP SOCKET (bench_server.sh STREAM=1 vs 0): only ~1.5% gap (87.7 vs 89.0),\n  stddev ~0.1 -\u003e the channel/writer ARCHITECTURE is nearly free. Confirms the\n  terminal (render/flow-control) is the cause, not the design.\n\nTOOLING: `bench_server.sh` = the CLEAN terminal-free measurement env (TCP client\ndrains fast, very low variance). Use it for streaming-cost + decode-throughput\nA/Bs instead of terminal runs. `elapsed` in llama-chat Main.hx is taken AFTER\nwriter.join(), so a slow tty inflates wall-tok/s even if decode is decoupled.\n\nFIX (COMMITTED 5cd78d27, validated coherent but NOT terminal-validated):\nMain.hx `Channel(256)`-\u003e`Channel(0)` (unbounded, send never blocks) + writer\ndrain-coalesce (receive then tryReceive loop -\u003e one Sys.print, fewer/larger tty\nwrites). Also added Q4Matmul.setPoolProfile(code override; env NUE_POOL_PROFILE\nstill wins) as a design API — the user clarified cooperative/P-1 is NOT the\nstreaming fix. Still needs the user's terminal to confirm it closes the ~10 tok/s.\nRelated: [[feature_extern_method_bodies_and_env_split]], [[perf_haxe_kernel_pool_cooperation_blocker]].\n\nUser's 3-run bench showed stddev 7.8 (vs 0.39 prior) and suspected the channel fixes, then deopt.\nControlled 3-variant x6-subprocess A/B: (a) CHANNEL SWEEP EXONERATED — pre-sweep vs HEAD bundle\nwithin noise (81.0 vs 83.3 median), and the user's bench had run a PRE-fix bundle anyway (rzb\n14:24 vs commits 22:5x — ALWAYS check bundle mtime vs commits before attributing perf to a code\nchange); (b) stddev 7.8 did NOT reproduce (1.9-3.5 across 18 runs; 3-run samples on a loaded box\nlie); (c) DEOPT theory right in direction: promote=true best on every metric (84.53/1.96/8.72s).\nPOLICY (user): START_INTERPRETED=false + TIER_PROMOTION=true — cranelift functions MUST be able\nto promote to LLVM; frozen tiers turn any lazily-compiled request-path function into permanent\nper-request variance (short responses expose it; long gens amortize it — why yesterday's 0.39\nlooked fine with promote=false). RESIDUAL: first_rtt ~9s on ALL variants for tiny responses\n(ready=3.3s) — first-request cold path (lazy compile marathon or first prefill) unexplained;\nname it with RAYZOR_TIER_TRACE_STARTUP=1 / RAYZOR_LLVM_TRACE_STARTUP=1.\n\nDecomposition of first_rtt 8.7-9.3s: (1) stream:false makes first_byte = FULL COMPLETION of a\n500-800-token gen (~6-9s @ ~83 tps) — presentation, not perf; STREAM=1 shows true TTFB 0.30s req1 /\n0.25s steady (prefill_med 269ms + RTT), cold_ttft 14s -\u003e 3.97s, tok/s unchanged (~82). (2) the old\npromote=false added a REAL ~24% req1 tax (69.9 vs 91.5); with promotion ON the same-prompt req1 tax\nis only ~4% (79.9 vs 83.3). (3) req_s spread at temp=0.5 is mostly GENERATION-LENGTH variance\n(bytes 2277-3754 across identical prompts). Per-token health: p95 13.4ms p99 15.5ms max 31ms single\nspike — no deopt/compile stalls mid-serving. Remaining cold costs (minor): ready ~3.7s (model load\n1.8s dominates) + ~4% req1 tps. Bench reading rule: with stream:false, first_byte/cold_ttft are\ncompletion-latency numbers, NOT responsiveness — use STREAM=1 for TTFB claims.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_streaming_gap_terminal_bound` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}